@*
    Task 8.4 — the ASP.NET Core replacement for Areas/Admin/Views/Web.config's
    <system.web.webPages.razor><pages> element (Requirement 4.4).

    THIS FILE IS THE SINGLE BIGGEST LEVER IN TASK 8.4, and the effect is measured, not
    asserted: adding it ALONE — no other edit — took Nop.Admin from 590 errors to 326, and
    took the CS0246 "<X>Model could not be found" family from 504 to ZERO. Note the
    headline count fell by less than 504 because Roslyn only binds a view's method body
    once its declarations resolve, so ~213 body-level CS0103 errors became visible for the
    first time in the same build (the phenomenon task 7.3 recorded at §27, where Nop.Web
    went 43 -> 1974 mid-task). A falling count is not the measure; the CS0246 family going
    to zero is.

    Areas/Admin/Views/Web.config declared two things every view in this tree depended on:

      pageBaseType="Nop.Web.Framework.ViewEngines.Razor.WebViewPage"
          -> the @inherits directive below. This is what makes T("Admin.Some.Resource")
             resolve: the localizer lives on that base class. It is also what makes the
             ~500 CS0246 "<X>Model could not be found" errors go away — not because the
             models were broken (they compile fine in Models/, task 8.3 took that folder
             to zero), but because <namespaces> no longer applied inside views.

      <namespaces>          -> the @using directives below.

    WHY ONE FILE IS ENOUGH, and why it must be exactly here.
    Razor discovers _ViewImports.cshtml by walking UP from the view's OWN directory to the
    project root — the trap task 7.5 measured the hard way (deferral 7.5-1): Nop.Web's
    Views/_ViewImports.cshtml does NOT apply to Themes/DefaultClean/Views/, because Views/
    is not on that view's upward path. Nop.Admin has exactly ONE view tree,
    Areas/Admin/Views/ (with Shared/ and Shared/EditorTemplates/ beneath it - there is no
    Shared/DisplayTemplates/ in this project) and no Themes/ tree, so a single file at the
    tree root is reached from
    every one of the 325 views. Task 8.2 moved the tree here from Views/ to resolve
    deferral 8.1-4; this file moved with it in the sense that it is authored at the new
    root, not the old one.

    Areas/Admin/Views/Web.config is DELETED by this task, and the now-dead
    <Content Update="Areas\Admin\Views\Web.config" CopyToPublishDirectory="Never" />
    entry was removed from Nop.Admin.csproj rather than left as a no-op — exactly as task
    7.5 did for Nop.Web's two equivalents. Nothing on .NET 10 can read the file: its
    <configSections> group names System.Web.WebPages.Razor, its <pages> element names
    MVC 5 Web Forms view types, its <httpHandlers>/<handlers> register
    System.Web.HttpNotFoundHandler (whose job — refusing to serve .cshtml over HTTP — is
    now done by task 7.4's NopStaticFileProvider allow-list, which does not permit .cshtml
    and additionally denies the extension outright), and validateRequest="false" opted out
    of ASP.NET request validation, which does not exist at all (deferral 7.3-3).
*@
@inherits Nop.Web.Framework.ViewEngines.Razor.WebViewPage<TModel>

@*
    Direct translations of the four System.Web entries in the legacy <namespaces> list:
        System.Web.Mvc       -> Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc
        System.Web.Mvc.Html  -> Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Rendering (IHtmlHelper extensions)
        System.Web.Routing   -> Microsoft.AspNetCore.Routing
        System.Web.Mvc.Ajax  -> NO counterpart. The Ajax.* helpers do not exist in
                                ASP.NET Core, and no admin view used them — verified, zero
                                "Ajax." occurrences across all 325 .cshtml files. Dropped.

    Microsoft.AspNetCore.Html is added for IHtmlContent/HtmlString, which replace
    MvcHtmlString throughout (task 6.3 §15a/§15b).

    NOTE WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT: System.Web.Optimization. Bundling is dropped with no
    successor (design section 8), and task 8.1 verified Nop.Admin never bound to the API —
    zero occurrences under Administration/. The legacy list did not carry it either (unlike
    Nop.Web's, which had it purely for Razor IntelliSense), so this is not even a removal.
*@
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Html
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Rendering
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Routing

@*
    The Nop.Admin.Models.* list, carried over verbatim — all 29 namespaces, in the legacy
    order. Checked for the hazard task 7.3 hit in Nop.Web (importing both
    Nop.Web.Models.Boards and Nop.Web.Models.Catalog made SearchModel ambiguous and broke
    two Search.cshtml views): a script comparing every namespace-level public type across
    these 29 namespaces finds 188 distinct names and ZERO collisions. The six names that
    LOOK duplicated — ActivityLogModel, OrderModel, ProductModel, ProductAttributeModel,
    ProductAttributeValueModel, StoreModel — are all NESTED types (e.g.
    CustomerModel.OrderModel vs Orders.OrderModel), which a using directive does not bring
    into scope. So importing all 29 is safe, and it is what 3.90 did.
*@
@using Nop.Admin.Models.Affiliates
@using Nop.Admin.Models.Blogs
@using Nop.Admin.Models.Catalog
@using Nop.Admin.Models.Cms
@using Nop.Admin.Models.Common
@using Nop.Admin.Models.Customers
@using Nop.Admin.Models.Directory
@using Nop.Admin.Models.Discounts
@using Nop.Admin.Models.ExternalAuthentication
@using Nop.Admin.Models.Forums
